{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Say to the priests</b> — juxtaposed with the warning to priests not to defile themselves through the ov, in which too a warning was stated not to defile by it. Alternatively: that a priest not defile himself for the dead when he wishes to inquire of the ov, since one must defile himself when inquiring of it. Alternatively: for this reason 'Say to the priests' is juxtaposed to ov and yid'oni — if you say, from whom shall we know the future? Behold, it is not sought through ov and yid'oni; therefore 'Say to the priests' is juxtaposed to it — behold, you have a priest who shall inquire for you through Urim veTummim. And thus it is in Tanḥuma: 'A man or woman, when there is among them an ov or yid'oni' — this is Saul, and the woman possessed of an ov, and juxtaposed to it is 'Say to the priests.' Rabbi Levi said: It would have been fitting for him to inquire through Urim veTummim, stated below, and not through the ov, stated above. Above it is written 'their blood is in them,' and juxtaposed to it 'he shall not defile' — for if he defiles, his blood is in it. And that is what we say: a priest who served in impurity — his fellow priests split his skull." "<b>אל הכהנים</b> in gematria: לא חללים (not profaned ones)." "<b>בני אהרן</b> in gematria: זהו (this is it). Also בעלי מומין (blemished priests). Why 'sons of Aaron' and not 'daughters of Aaron'? Because they are stunted — death for all generations." "<b>אמור אל הכהנים וגו' ואמרת</b> in gematria: לדורות (for generations). And ואמרת — these are the letters of אמרו\"ת טהורות (pure sayings). אמור and ואמרת: the first utterance — so as not to defile for the dead; and the second utterance — to defile for a dead mitzvah." "<b>לנפש לא</b> in gematria: בדם המת (in the blood of the dead)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:20:17.697111892Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}